        "portalId": "a6216258-7def-43aa-8001-53536e5764e5",
        "title": "Lesson 17: ḤUDŪD – Retraction by the Confessor and Witness.",
        "overview": "In this lesson, we delves into the intricate legal and religious discussions surrounding the concept of \"Had of Rajm,\" which refers to the punishment of stoning in Islamic law. The discourse begins by explaining the conditions under which this punishment is applicable, particularly focusing on the concept of \"muhsan,\" a term used to describe a person who has been married and consummated the marriage. The speaker emphasizes that the punishment of stoning is not easily applied and requires very strict conditions, including the presence of four witnesses to the act of adultery or a confession made four times by the accused. The discussion also highlights the importance of repentance and the possibility of retracting a confession, which can lead to the dismissal of the punishment if there is doubt about the initial admission.\n\nThe lesson further explores the differentiation between a muhsan and a non-muhsan, explaining that a muhsan, due to their previous marital status and consummation, is liable for stoning if they commit adultery, whereas a non-muhsan would receive 100 lashes. The speaker provides various scenarios to illustrate these points, including the implications of marrying a non-Muslim or a slave, which affects the status of being a muhsan. The discourse also touches on the procedural aspects of carrying out the punishment, ensuring it is neither too harsh nor too lenient, and the role of the judiciary in administering these laws. The session concludes with a reflection on the gravity of the punishment of stoning, seen as a severe consequence for showing ingratitude to the divine blessings of marriage and lawful sexual relations.",
