        "portalId": "19b52f91-0176-476b-ba47-2ca47283b824",
        "title": "Lesson 43 | Oaths in Lawsuits",
        "overview": "In this lesson, we examine the intricacies of oaths in Islamic legal proceedings, particularly within the context of lawsuits. We begin by discussing the role of oaths, emphasizing that they are sworn by Allah and may include His attributes to reinforce their gravity. We also explore the circumstances in which oaths are generally not administered—such as in cases of divorce or the freeing of slaves—except in rare situations where the sanctity of Allah’s name is not being upheld in society. Additionally, we consider the administration of oaths to non-Muslims, such as Jews and Christians, and reflect on the historical context of Zoroastrianism, including the view that Zoroaster may have been a prophet whose teachings shared similarities with the Abrahamic traditions.\n\nFurthermore, in this lesson, we analyze the legal nuances of litigation, especially in disputes over ownership. We look at various scenarios in which a plaintiff claims ownership of property held by a defendant, and how the defendant’s response can either initiate or prevent formal litigation. We emphasize the central role of evidence and the responsibility of the judge in adjudicating such cases. We also explore the complexities surrounding oaths in matters of marriage, divorce, and usurpation, highlighting the importance of precise wording to avoid self-incrimination or false testimony. We conclude by reflecting on how these legal principles contribute to the preservation of justice and order within the community.",
