        "overview": "In this lesson, we explore the intricacies of testimony in Islamic law, focusing on who is considered credible to testify. We examine the distinction between major and minor sins and how they affect a person’s status as an upright (ʿadl) witness. We also discuss the testimony of individuals with heretical beliefs, differentiating between those whose beliefs take them outside of Islam and those who remain within its fold.\n\nAdditionally, we analyze the conditions under which non-Muslims (dhimmīs) may testify—specifically, that they can testify against one another but not against Muslims—emphasizing the central role of credibility and integrity in legal proceedings. We further highlight the requirement that testimony must align with the claim presented in court for it to be valid.\n\nFinally, we address the issue of perjury, outlining different scholarly views on how to deal with those who lie under oath. We underscore the importance of maintaining more good deeds than bad, abstaining from major sins, and preserving one’s upright character in order to retain the status of a credible witness.",
