        "overview": "In this lesson, we discuss the principles of testimony in Islamic law, with a focus on the importance of congruency between witnesses. We examine a scenario in which two sets of witnesses provide conflicting accounts regarding the location of a murder, illustrating that testimony must be consistent in order to be valid. We learn that when testimonies contradict one another, neither can be accepted, as this indicates that one party is not truthful. We also emphasize that Islamic jurisprudence requires at least two witnesses whose testimonies align, as a single witness is not sufficient.\n\nWe then explore certain exceptions to the general rule that a witness must have directly seen or heard the event they testify about. These exceptions include matters such as paternity, death, marriage, and the authority of a judge, where testimony may be based on widespread knowledge or reliable transmission from others. We also study the concept of shahādah ʿalā al-shahādah, where a witness may delegate their testimony to another individual if they are unable to attend court due to a valid excuse.\n\nFinally, we conclude with a brief introduction to the next topic, which will address the retraction of testimonies.",
