        "overview": "In this lesson, we explore the legal implications of retracting testimony in Islamic law by examining two primary scenarios. We begin with the case where witnesses retract their testimony before a judge has issued a verdict. In this situation, we note that the retraction is accepted, the testimony is rendered void, and the witnesses are not held liable, since no ruling was based on their statements and no harm has occurred.\n\nWe then move to the second scenario, where witnesses retract their testimony after a verdict has already been passed. Here, we explain that the original judgment remains in place, but the witnesses are held liable for any harm or damages caused by their testimony. This approach preserves the integrity of the legal system while ensuring accountability.\n\nIn this lesson, we also examine practical examples—such as financial disputes and marriage contracts—to demonstrate how liability is assessed depending on the timing and consequences of the retraction. We highlight the principle that the testimony of those who remain is given precedence over those who withdraw, ensuring that the required evidentiary standard is upheld. Overall, we reflect on how Islamic jurisprudence carefully balances justice with responsibility in these matters.",
