        "portalId": "3e175d6e-2acc-481b-a600-ef496c386240",
        "title": "Lesson 52 | ĀDĀB AL-QĀḌĪ – CONDUCT OF THE JUDGE",
        "overview": "In this class, the discussion revolves around the intricacies of Islamic legal principles, particularly focusing on the concepts of testimony and judgment. The session begins with an exploration of the consequences of retracted testimonies in cases of murder and adultery. It highlights the importance of witness credibility and the implications of their testimonies on legal outcomes. The class delves into scenarios where witnesses retract their statements after a judgment has been passed, examining the liabilities and consequences for both primary and secondary witnesses. The discussion emphasizes the distinction between effective causes and conditions in legal rulings, illustrating how these principles apply to various cases, including those involving false testimonies and the appointment of judges.\nThe latter part of the class transitions into the responsibilities and qualifications of judges within the Islamic legal framework. It outlines the conditions necessary for someone to be appointed as a judge, emphasizing the importance of integrity, knowledge, and the ability to perform independent juristic reasoning (ijtihad). The session underscores the significance of appointing the most capable individuals to judicial positions to ensure justice and uphold the rights of individuals. The class concludes with a reflection on the role of judges as representatives of divine justice, tasked with resolving disputes and delivering rights to the rightful parties, drawing parallels to the practices of the Prophet Muhammad and his companions.",
