        "overview": "In this lesson, we explore the responsibilities and ethical guidelines for judges in Islamic jurisprudence. We examine the importance of maintaining impartiality and integrity, emphasizing that judges must avoid accepting gifts or attending private invitations that could compromise their neutrality. We also distinguish between general and specific invitations, noting that judges should avoid gatherings where their presence is the primary reason for the event, as this may lead to perceptions of bias. At the same time, we highlight the importance of judges remaining engaged in the community through activities such as attending funerals and visiting the sick, reinforcing their broader social role.\n\nWe then study the process of litigation, focusing on the necessity of treating all litigants equally regardless of their social standing. We discuss procedures related to credit transactions, emphasizing that a judge should not hastily detain a debtor without clear evidence of deliberate delay in repayment. We also examine cases involving written testimony from another jurisdiction, outlining the conditions under which such documentation may be accepted. Finally, we consider the role of women in the judiciary, recognizing their capacity to serve as judges in most cases, with the exception of those involving severe prescribed punishments.",
