        "portalId": "700eb7fd-7d70-4adc-b63c-68a3c39bdb54",
        "title": "Lesson 10: The I’rāb of The Ism",
        "overview": "In this video, we continue with our discussion binā. The default in asmāʾ (nouns) is iʿrāb, while in ḥurūf (particles) it is bināʾ. For afʿāl (verbs), the Kūfiyyūn argue their origin is iʿrāb, but the Baṣriyyūn say they only resemble asmāʾ and thus carry iʿrāb secondarily. Asmāʾ are of two types:\n- Muʿrab (the original state): their endings change with ʿawāmil, either visibly (زيدٌ / زيدًا) or by assumption (موسى / الفتى).\n- Mabnī (secondary state): their endings remain fixed, usually due to resemblance to ḥurūf in one of four ways: (1) form, (2) meaning, (3) dependence, or (4) usage.\n\nAmong the mabnī nouns are: pronouns, asmāʾ al-ishārah (demonstratives), asmāʾ al-mawṣūlah (relatives), asmāʾ al-sharṭ (conditionals), asmāʾ al-istifhām (interrogatives), and ism al-fiʿl. Some nouns like أيّ can be partly muʿrab when used as muḍāf. Scholars gave additional reasons for bināʾ, but most trace back to resemblance with ḥurūf.",
