        "portalId": "9a96fa84-9c17-47ca-985e-a8355049ddcf",
        "title": "Lesson 11: The Mabnī Asmā",
        "overview": "In this video, we discuss the mabnī asmā and af’āl in more detail. Mabnī nouns appear on one of four endings: sukūn (e.g. كم), fatḥ (أين), kasr (أمس), or ḍamm (حيث). The default is sukūn, and other ḥarakāt occur for specific reasons. Scholars list causes such as: avoiding two sukūns meeting, inability to pronounce a lone consonant, needing a vowel to begin speech, partial roots in iʿrāb (like قبلُ), resemblance to muʿrab forms, or phonetic balance in words like هو.\nExamples illustrate these rules: كم (questioning vs. takthīr), أين (fatḥ for lightness with hamzah), أمس (built on kasr when used as “yesterday” with conditions like no alif-lām or plural), and حيث (built on ḍamm since it is always muḍāf to a sentence, though Kūfans allow exceptions). Each vowel has phonological or grammatical motivations: fatḥ for ease, kasr for breaking sukūn or marking femininity, ḍamm for resemblance to plurals or cut-off iḍāfah.\nAs for afʿāl, the Baṣriyyūn hold the default is bināʾ, making the māḍī and amr mabnī and the muḍāriʿ exceptional for iʿrāb. Kūfans argue the default is iʿrāb, with amr derived from muḍāriʿ. For the māḍī, its bināʾ is usually on fatḥ, but with wāw al-jamāʿah it takes ḍamm, and with a moving pronoun it takes sukūn (ضربوا / ضربْتُ).",
