        "portalId": "afae5100-cec4-4cf9-9dbf-56c1028767f9",
        "title": "Lesson 21 : The Ġayr Munṣarif Ism Continued",
        "overview": "In this video we continue our discussion on the ġayr munṣarif ism.\n\n‘Adl: A noun shifts from its original form without new meaning. It’s of two types:\nTaḥqīqī: real change, like مثنى (from اثنان اثنان).\n\nTaqdīrī: assumed in names like عمر, treated as altered to supply a second cause.\n\nTa’nīth is of three kinds.\nBy alif: mamdūdah (صحراء), maqṣūrah (حبلى). Each counts as two causes.\n\nBy tā’: works with ‘alamiyyah (طلحة, فاطمة).\n\nBy meaning: works with ‘alamiyyah under conditions (e.g., سعاد, سقر, جور).\n\nTa‘rīf: (proper names) cause non-declension when paired with others (wazn al-fi‘l, ‘adl, etc.).\nTarkīb: compound names (بعلبك) become non-declinable only with ‘alamiyyah.\nAlif + nūn: block ṣarf in names (عثمان) or adjectives that don’t feminize with tā (سكران → سكرى).\n‘Ujmah: foreign names like إبراهيم, if longer than three letters and used as names.\nWaṣf: prevents ṣarf with:\n‘Adl (مثنى),\n\nAlif+nūn (سكران),\n\nWazn al-fi‘l on أفعل (أحمر → حمراء).",
