        "portalId": "9c4ab473-273a-45b1-a180-f85708d7e248",
        "title": "Lesson 23 : The Pronoun and its Types",
        "overview": "In this video we discuss the different types of pronouns in Arabic. The pronoun (المضمر/الضمير) is an ism for the first, second, or third person, and is either hidden or apparent. The hidden has no spoken form, either necessarily (as in أقم, أقوم, نقوم, صه, ما أحسن زيدًا, خلا زيدًا) or optionally (زيد يقوم). The apparent has a form, and is either attached (متصل) — which cannot begin speech nor follow إلا, and appears as marfūʿ, manṣūb, or majrūr (e.g., ضربتُ، أكرمني، مر بي) — or detached (منفصل), which can begin speech or follow إلا. Detached pronouns are twelve marfūʿ (أنا، نحن، هو…) used as mubtada, fāʿil, etc., and twelve manṣūb (إياي، إياك…) used only as mafʿūl bihi, like {إياك نعبد}.\nAll pronouns are mabnī. Normally the attached is used unless impossible, except in cases like سلنيه/سلني إياه, كنتَه/كنتَ إياه, or with verbs taking two objects. When two pronouns meet, the more specific precedes, as in {أنلزمكموها}.",
