        "overview": "In this video we discuss the ‘alam. The ‘alam is the only type of ma‘rifah definite by itself. It is of two kinds: shakhṣiyy for individuals (زيد، مكة) and jinsiyy for genera (أُسامة for lion). Though jinsiyy resembles nakirah in meaning, it is treated as ma‘rifah.\nIt divides into ism (زيد), kunyah (أبو بكر), and laqab (زين العابدين, بطّة). If ism and laqab meet, the ism comes first; if one is compound, the laqab follows as a tābi‘.\nAn ‘alam is either mufrad (زيد، هند) or murakkab, of three types: iḍāfī (عبد الله), mazjī (بعلبك, ending in -ويه mabnī on kasr, otherwise ghayr munṣarif), and isnādī (برق نهرُه), where the second part stays as ḥikāyah. Names like أبو بكر allow normal i‘rāb or ḥikāyah.",
