        "title": "Lesson 37 : The Lā That Governs Like Inna",
        "overview": "In this video, we discuss the lā ‘āmilah ‘amala laysa. The lā used to negate an entire class means denying the existence of anything from that group, like لا رجلَ في الدار — “There is no man in the house.”\nIt acts like inna, giving naṣb to its ism and raf‘ to its khabar, if both are nakirah and the ism directly follows lā.\nIts ism can be:\nMuḍāf or shabīh bi-l-muḍāf, both manṣūb.\n\nMufrad, which is mabnī: on fatḥ for singular/broken plural, on yā’ for dual/masculine plural, and on kasrah for feminine plural.\n\nWhen lā repeats, as in لا حولَ ولا قوةَ, the first can have fatḥ or raf‘; the second can vary depending on the first.\nIf the ism has a ṣifah directly after it, like لا رجلَ ظريفٌ / ظريفًا, the na‘t may be fatḥ, naṣb, or raf‘.\nIf the khabar is known, it’s usually omitted (e.g. لا حول ولا قوة). If unknown, it must be mentioned (لا أحدَ أغيرُ من الله).\nWhen lā enters a ma‘rifah or is separated from its ism, it loses its ‘amal, as in لا زيدُ في الدار ولا عمرو.",
