        "overview": "In our recent discussion, we delved into the intricate concepts of \"Amr\" and \"Nahi\" within Islamic jurisprudence, exploring their definitions and implications. We began by examining \"Amr,\" which signifies a positive command demanding action, and contrasted it with \"Nahi,\" the negation of an action, urging the abandonment of certain behaviors. Through various examples, we illustrated how \"Nahi\" is expressed verbally by someone in authority, implying an obligation to refrain from specific actions. This exploration included discussions on the different forms and contexts in which \"Nahi\" is applied, such as prohibitions against harming others or engaging in unethical practices, and how these prohibitions are rooted in the principles of justice and morality.\n\nFurthermore, we explored the nuanced interpretations of \"Nahi\" and its implications in different scenarios, emphasizing the importance of understanding the context and intent behind prohibitions. We discussed how \"Nahi\" can be for \"Tahreem\" (prohibition) or \"Karaha\" (dislike), depending on the presence of supporting evidence or \"Daleel.\" This distinction is crucial in determining whether an action is inherently forbidden or merely discouraged. Additionally, we examined the concept of \"Qubh,\" or the intrinsic negativity associated with prohibited actions, and how it can be understood through both rational and Sharia-based perspectives. By analyzing these concepts, we aimed to provide a comprehensive understanding of the role of \"Nahi\" in guiding ethical behavior and maintaining social harmony.",
