        "overview": "The science of Uṣūl al-Fiqh (أُصُولُ الفِقْهِ) provides the methodological foundation through which Islamic legal rulings are derived from their primary sources. Among its central mechanisms is qiyās (قِيَاس), analogical reasoning, which extends rulings from established cases to new ones based upon a shared effective cause (ʿillah — عِلَّة).\nAfter mastering the structure of analogy—its aṣl (الأَصْل, original case), farʿ (الفَرْع, new case), ḥukm (الحُكْم, ruling), and ʿillah (effective cause)—jurists turn to an equally important question: How is an analogy refuted? This discipline is known as dafʿ al-qiyās (دَفْعُ القِيَاس), the systematic refutation of analogy.\nThis subject is not merely theoretical. Differences in identifying the ʿillah were among the primary reasons for the development of distinct legal schools (madhāhib — مَذَاهِب). Later jurists refined these refutation techniques into a structured discipline known as ʿilm al-munāẓarah wa al-jadal (عِلْمُ المُنَاظَرَةِ وَالجَدَل), the science of formal debate.\nThis lesson examines the methodological ways in which an analogy may be challenged, corrected, or overturned.",
        "topic": ""
