        "overview": "This lesson continues the study of uṣūl al-fiqh after completing the discussion on qiyās (قِيَاس), legal analogy. Having examined the concept of ʿillah (عِلَّة) in depth during the study of qiyās, it becomes necessary to revisit and clarify several related foundational concepts: sabab (سَبَب), sharṭ (شَرْط), and māniʿ (مَانِع).\nThese terms are central to understanding how legal rulings (aḥkām, أَحْكَام) operate within Islamic law. They explain how rulings are triggered, what conditions are required for them to exist, and what may prevent them from taking effect.\nThe jurists state:\nاِعْلَمْ أَنَّ الْحُكْمَ كَمَا يَثْبُتُ بِعِلَّتِهِ فَإِنَّهُ يَرْتَبِطُ أَيْضًا بِسَبَبِهِ وَشَرْطِهِ وَيُمْنَعُ بِمَانِعِهِ\n“Know that just as a ruling is established by its effective cause, it is also connected to its cause, its condition, and may be prevented by an obstacle.”\nUnderstanding these relationships allows the student to grasp how Islamic law functions in practical reality.\nAfter completing the discussion of al-adillah al-muttafaq ʿalayhā (الأدلة المتفق عليها), the agreed-upon legal evidences, the study of uṣūl al-fiqh proceeds to al-adillah al-mukhtalaf fīhā (الأدلة المختلف فيها), the evidences over which scholars differ regarding their authority. These are proofs that some jurists accept as valid sources of law, while others reject or restrict.\nAmong the most prominent of these disputed evidences is al-Istihsān (الاِسْتِحْسَان), a principle strongly associated with the Ḥanafī school. Because Istihsān is closely connected to Qiyās (قِيَاس), legal analogy, it is studied immediately after Qiyās in the traditional sequence of uṣūl.\nThis lesson examines the meaning of Istihsān, its types, its relationship to Qiyās, and the conditions under which it is preferred or rejected. Through detailed examples, we see the precision of classical juristic reasoning and the methodological depth of Ḥanafī legal theory.",
